<div class="help-article">
    <p class="help-lead">
        The <a href="./">Dashboard</a> is the summary: what came in, what went out, what's left over and what you're worth, then
        where it went, by month and by category. Click anything to see the transactions behind it.
    </p>
    <HelpClip Name="dashboard-drilldown" Caption="Clicking a month, then a category and its details." />

    <h3>Choosing what it covers</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Period</strong> (top right): this month, last month, the last 3 or 12 months, year to date, or last year.</li>
        <li><strong>Accounts</strong>: with more than one account, tick the ones to include. None ticked means all of them. The choice is
            remembered.</li>
        <li><strong>Tags</strong>: pick tags to see only those transactions, such as <em>Rental</em> for a rental property's income and
            costs. See <a href="help?a=tags">Tags</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The four numbers</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Income</strong>: money in, other than transfers and refunds.</li>
        <li><strong>Spending</strong>: money out, less refunds. Transfers between your accounts and credit card payments are left out, so
            a purchase isn't counted once on the card and again when you pay the card.</li>
        <li><strong>Net cash flow</strong>: income minus spending, with the share of income you kept (your savings rate).</li>
        <li><strong>Net worth</strong>: what your accounts hold, plus your properties' estimated values, minus what you owe on cards,
            lines of credit and loans, from each account's current balance. It always covers every account, whatever tags are
            picked. With accounts picked, a property counts when one of its loans is among them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Everything is in Canadian dollars. Income and spending in an account kept in another currency (a US dollar card, say) are
        converted at the Bank of Canada's average rate for the month they happened in, or at the latest rate for the month under
        way, so a past month's figures don't move when the dollar does. LoonieBooks fetches those monthly rates for your accounts'
        currencies (only the currency is sent).
    </p>
    <p>
        With investment holdings, a line under the totals says when their prices were last updated from stocktreemap.com (the
        time of stocktreemap's prices, and when LoonieBooks last checked) and how many holdings were updated. A yellow note lists
        any that weren't, with the reason: a mutual fund code, a symbol the price list doesn't carry, a price that doesn't look
        right for the holding, or one stocktreemap hasn't updated for days. Those holdings keep their statement's value.
        <span class="help-ui">Dismiss</span> hides the note until the list changes. See
        <a href="help?a=holdings">Holdings</a> for how prices are matched.
    </p>

    <h3>By month</h3>
    <p>
        The chart shows income against spending for each month. Click a month to list its transactions in the panel on the
        right; click it again, or the month's × button above the list, to clear it.
    </p>
    <p>
        The <span class="help-ui">‹</span> and <span class="help-ui">›</span> buttons on either side of the chart (or a swipe
        across it, on a phone) switch to <strong>Net worth by month</strong>: your net worth at the end of each month, with how
        much it went up or down over the period beside the title. Hover over a month for what's in it, or click it to list its
        transactions, as on the other chart. The chart you picked is remembered.
    </p>
    <p>
        LoonieBooks keeps only each account's latest balance, so earlier months are worked back from it: an account's balance at
        the end of a month is its current balance less what its transactions added since (up to the date the balance was set).
        That makes it an estimate. Investments change with their transactions and, for listed stocks and ETFs in their holdings,
        with each listing's price at the end of the month from stocktreemap.com (the holdings count as held in the same
        quantities throughout); other holdings, such as mutual funds and GICs, change only with their transactions. Only the
        months the chart shows are downloaded, each once, and nothing about your holdings is sent.
        Foreign-currency accounts are converted at today's rates; and each property counts at its value carried to that month by
        its area's house price index (backwards too, before the month its value is from), or as entered without one.
        A closed account counts what it held before its last transactions. Like the Net worth number, it follows the accounts
        you picked but not tags, and its last month matches that number.
    </p>
    <p>
        One more step switches to <strong>Account value by month</strong>: one account's balance at the end of each month, in
        the account's own currency. Pick the account from the list beside the title (it offers the accounts the dashboard
        covers), or turn the mouse wheel over the chart to step through them; the one you picked is remembered. It's worked back the same way as net worth, and an investment account moves
        with its listings' month-end prices too: then the change beside the title says <em>estimated</em> (hover over it for
        how), since it's not your broker's figures. For a loan, line of credit or card, the line is what's owed. An account
        without a balance has nothing to work back from: set its balance on the <a href="accounts">Accounts</a> page first.
    </p>
    <p>
        Two charts measure spending against the budgets you set on the <a href="budget">Budget</a> page. In both, each column is
        as tall as the budget or the spending, whichever is more: green is spending within the budget, faded green is what's left
        of it, red is spending over it, and a short line marks where spending would be by today at an even pace. Hover over a
        column for the numbers. Budgets are the household's, so these charts cover every account and tag, whatever the dashboard
        is narrowed to. See <a href="help?a=budget">Budget</a>.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Budget vs spending by month</strong>, the step after income and spending: each month of the period against a
            twelfth of its year's budget, leaving out budgets kept for the year only (tuition, tax instalments), which a month's
            tooltip lists instead, with the categories that went over and spending no budget covers. Pick a category in the
            Spending list first and it shows that category's budget. Click a month to list its transactions.</li>
        <li><strong>Budget status</strong>, the step after that: one column per budgeted category, largest budget first, each
            drawn against its own budget (100%), so a small budget reads as clearly as a large one and red past 100% is over. The
            amounts are in each column's tooltip, and what was spent without a budget is beside the legend. <span class="help-ui">Month</span>
            compares the month you picked (or the period's last) with each category's monthly budget; yearly-only budgets
            aren't in it. <span class="help-ui">Year</span> compares that year so far with each category's whole budget, yearly
            ones included. Click a category to list its transactions, as in the Spending list. Month or Year is remembered.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>By category or account, and your holdings</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>The buttons at the top left of the card pick what it lists: <strong>Spending</strong> or <strong>Income</strong> ranks the
            period's categories, <strong>Assets</strong> ranks your investment holdings by market value and your real estate by
            estimated value, and <strong>Liabilities</strong> ranks what you owe. Bars or Pie, at the bottom
            left, switches the list between bars and a pie chart. These choices are remembered.</li>
        <li>The list's total is at the bottom right: the period's spending or income (and the month, when you've picked one), or
            what your assets are worth and what you owe, in Canadian dollars. Hover over it for what it counts.</li>
        <li><strong>By category</strong> or <strong>By account</strong>, at the top right, picks how Spending and Income are added
            up: by category, or by the account the money came in to or went out of (your credit card, your chequing account…).
            Refunds reduce spending on the account they came back to, and transfers between your accounts are left out. Accounts
            aren't clickable; to see one account's transactions, pick it under <strong>Accounts</strong>. Amounts are in Canadian
            dollars, other currencies at their month's rate.</li>
        <li><strong>Assets</strong> values your investment holdings in Canadian dollars, from each account's latest import on
            <a href="holdings">Holdings</a>. <strong>By holding</strong> adds up each holding (by symbol) across your accounts;
            <strong>By account</strong> adds up each account's holdings instead (the same button as for Spending and Income). It follows the accounts you picked, but not the
            period or tags: it's where things stand now, like net worth.</li>
        <li><strong>Liabilities</strong> ranks what you owe. <strong>By property</strong> adds up the loans secured by each property
            (with the loans as its details), and puts cards and other unsecured debts under <em>Not secured by a property</em>;
            <strong>By account</strong> lists each loan, line of credit and card. Balances are in Canadian dollars, as last set on each
            account on <a href="accounts">Accounts</a>. A balance in your favour (a credit on a line or card) isn't shown as a bar
            but lowers what's owed.</li>
        <li>Under <strong>Assets</strong>, your properties are one more row, <strong>Real estate</strong>, at their estimated values
            (carried forward by their areas' house price index), with each property as its details. Click a property there to see in
            the panel how its value was worked out, the loans
            secured by it, its equity and its loan-to-value. Properties are kept on
            <a href="accounts">Accounts</a>.</li>
        <li>Click a holding (By holding) and the panel on the right shows where it's held instead of transactions: each account
            that holds it, with the units, price, market value, book value and gain there, and its share of the holding and of
            the account. Click an account's name to open it on <a href="holdings">Holdings</a>.</li>
        <li>Click an account (By account) to open its holdings beneath it, like a category's details (as a pie, they get a
            second, smaller pie), and list them in the panel with their figures. Click a holding there, or under the account,
            to see where else it's held.</li>
        <li>Click a category to list its transactions and open its details (Food &amp; Dining › Groceries, Restaurants…) beneath
            it; click a detail to narrow further. The arrow beside a category opens or closes its details without
            selecting it. One category is open at a time.</li>
        <li>As a pie, the open category gets a second, smaller pie under the first, showing how it divides into its details,
            each in a colour of its own (the largest keeps the category's colour). Click a slice there to pick that detail.</li>
        <li>Pick a category before a month and the chart switches to that category, month by month.</li>
        <li>Deposits nobody has categorised count as income until you file them as Salary, Business Income or Other Income.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The transaction panel</h3>
    <p>
        Shows the latest transactions, or those for the month and category you clicked. You can change a category or add a tag
        right there. A transaction that no longer fits stays in place until you press ↻; <span class="help-ui">See full list →</span>
        opens the same selection on <a href="transactions">Transactions</a>. With a holding or an investment account picked under
        <strong>Assets</strong>, or a property under its <strong>Real estate</strong> row, it shows that instead; its × button, or clicking a
        month, brings the transactions back.
    </p>
</div>
